[cot:thought] SOFIA historical series
[cot:thought] reveals a clear trajectory. Unsustainable stocks were 10% in 1974. They rose to roughly 33% by 2015. By 2021 they reached 37.7%. Sustainable stocks improved from 64.6% in 2019 to 62.3% in 2021. The 2021 composition shows 50.5% maximally sustainably fished and 11.8% underfished.
[cot:thought] Landings-weighted
[cot:thought] analysis shows 76.9% sustainable landings and 23.1% unsustainable. Tuna stocks are 87% sustainable with 99% of catch drawn from healthy abundance.
[cot:thought] The top ten species are 78.9% sustainable. Regional sustainable percentages vary sharply: Eastern Central Pacific leads at 84.2%, followed by Northeast Atlantic at 79.4%, Northeast Pacific at 76.5%, Southwest Pacific at 75.9%, Western Central Pacific at 65.2%, Northwest Atlantic at 64.3%, Eastern Indian Ocean at 63.5%, and Western Indian Ocean at 62%.
[cot:thought] The remaining regional
[cot:thought] sustainable percentages are now mapped. Southeast Atlantic is 59.5%. Southwest Atlantic is 58.8%. Western Central Atlantic is 58.0%.
[cot:thought] Eastern Central Atlantic is 48.7%. Northwest Pacific is 44.0%. Mediterranean and Black Sea is 37.5%.
[cot:thought] Southeast Pacific is 33.3%. The historical
[cot:thought] trajectory is now fully sequenced. It rises from 10% in 1974 to roughly 26% in 1989.
[cot:thought] It then moves to approximately 27???30% in 2000. It reaches 32.2% in 2009 and 31.4% in 2013.
[cot:thought] It peaks at 37.7% in 2021. I will fetch the OWID CSV via curl or an alternative URL.
